<handout>
    <title>
        Proving <m>A\subseteq B</m>: The <q>Choose an Element Method</q>
    </title>

    <p>
        The notation <m>A\subseteq B</m> is read
        <q> <m>A</m> is a subset of
            <m>B</m> </q> and means that if
        <m>x\in A</m> then <m>x\in B</m>, that is, every element of <m>A</m> is
        also an element of <m>B</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
        Thus, if we want to prove that <m>A\subseteq B</m>, we are proving a
        conditional statement, i.e., an <q>if - then</q> statement.
        We'll do a direct proof of this conditional statement.
        So, we'll assume the hypothesis, and show the conclusion.
    </p>

    <p>
        More specifically, we'll start with an arbitrary <m>x</m> such that
        <m>x\in A</m>, and we'll show <m>x\in B</m>.
        Let's consider the following theorem.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-congruence-subset2">
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>A = \{x\in\mathbb{Z} \mid x \equiv 2 \pmod{4}\} </m> and
                <m>B =\{x\in\mathbb{Z} \mid x \equiv 0 \pmod{2}\}</m> then
                <m>A\subseteq B</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <activity>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Prove <xref ref="thm-congruence-subset2"/>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="4in">
            <p>
                Start with a pre-proof analysis.
                Consider listing the elements in <m>A</m> and the elements in
                <m>B</m> to see if you believe the conjecture.
                Then, choose an arbitrary element of <m>A</m>, e.g.,
                <q> Let
                    <m>x\in A</m>.
                    So <m>x\in \mathbb{Z}</m> and <m>x\equiv 2 \pmod{4}</m>.</q>
                Work to show that this <m>x</m> is in <m>B</m>, that is
                <m>x\equiv 0 \pmod {2}</m>.
            </p>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2.5in">
            <p>
                (If time:) It's actually true that <m>A\subset B</m>.
                What would you add to your proof to prove this?
            </p>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <remark>
        <p>
            To say <m>A = B</m> is to say that <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> contain
            <em>exactly</em> the same elements.
            Said differently, it means these two things:
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Every element in <m>A</m> is also in <m>B</m> (which
                        means <m>A\subseteq B</m> ), and
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Every element in <m>B</m> is also in <m>A</m> (which
                        means <m>B\subseteq A</m> ).
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
            Indeed, a slick way to prove that <m>A=B</m> is to prove both
            <m>A\subseteq B</m> and <m>B\subseteq A</m>, both of which can be
            done using the <q>choose an element</q> method.
        </p>
    </remark>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-congruence-equal">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The sets <m>A = \{5k+11: k\in\mathbb{Z} \}</m> and
                <m>B = \{x\in\mathbb{Z}: x\equiv 1\pmod{5}\}</m> are equal.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <activity workspace="4in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Prove <xref ref="thm-congruence-equal"/>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>
</handout>